    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d713">

                <pb facs="0622" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg610"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.6511/132: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-30T18:00:00-04:00">August 30, 1940—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>278. Your 425, August 26, 3 p.m.<anchor n="44" xml:id="d713-f1"/> Steel
                    Commission has had a number of conversations with Jesse Jones and Warren
                    Pierson, and with various private companies. Delay in definite action is due
                    principally to fact that Congress has not yet passed Bill authorizing 500
                    million dollar increase for Export-Import Bank and removing 20 million dollar
                    limitation. The Department and Jones and Pierson are making every effort to push
                    Bill and it is expected it will be acted upon by the Senate soon. If it is
                    enacted, negotiations should proceed rapidly towards definite agreement.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d713-f1" n="44" xml:id="d713-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; the Ambassador in Brazil asked for information as to the progress of
                    steel plant negotiations (832.6511/133).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
